2.$ Applying the above formula with $x=2$ and $h=4$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{g(2+4)-g(2)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(6)-g(2)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-8}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5d7ce2f639b79390f91ae6ef13ae0b13f19ceb5826a85548464a4ed1610ea55"} {"question" "q-47387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/assets/c51e6206f77b63181f6c9f67b116ba5c0024886c061e8c15fe10edab87c87e03.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=3$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${f}^{′}(3)≈$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=3.$ Applying the above formula with $x=3$ and $h=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{f(3+2)-f(3)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(5)-f(3)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-5}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5d7ce2f639b79390f91ae6ef13ae0b13f19ceb5826a85548464a4ed1610ea55"} {"question" "q-342037" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342037" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/assets/1534153e6a5d79400a038849b48583d2ec9a62db1fc1f52a491b751e46861f51.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=g(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=0$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x+h)-g(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=0.$ Applying the above formula with $x=0$ and $h=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(0) & ≈\\frac{g(0+2)-g(0)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(2)-g(0)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-10}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{2} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5d7ce2f639b79390f91ae6ef13ae0b13f19ceb5826a85548464a4ed1610ea55"} {"question" "q-47388" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47388" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "-6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47388" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A hiker’s elevation was recorded over time for $6$ hours, starting from time $t=0$ hours. The following table shows the elevation $E(t)$, in meters, recorded at time $t.$\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $E(t)$ | $0$ | $200$ | $310$ | $395$ | $450$ | $525$ | $600$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change of elevation at time $t=1$ using a forward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\nAt $t=1,$ the estimated rate of change is {{field-1}} meters per hour." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{E}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{E(t+h)-E(t)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $t=1.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{E(1+1)-E(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{E(2)-E(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{310-200}{1} \\\\  & =110.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, at $t=1$ the rate of change was approximately $110$ meters per hour." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5d7ce2f639b79390f91ae6ef13ae0b13f19ceb5826a85548464a4ed1610ea55"} {"question" "q-342040" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342040" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "110"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Andrey wants to estimate his running speed. Andrey's friend Max takes note of Andrey's position while he is on the track. Max's observations are given in the table below, where $s(t)$ is the distance covered, measured in meters, $t$ seconds after Andrey started running.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $0$ | $4$ | $9$ | $15$ | $20$ | $27$ |\n\nApproximate Andrey's speed at the point where $t=6$ seconds using a forward difference approximation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{s}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{s(t+h)-s(t)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $t=6.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{′}(6) & ≈\\frac{s(6+2)-s(6)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(8)-s(6)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{20-15}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at $t=6$ Andrey's speed is approximately $2.5\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47378" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5d7ce2f639b79390f91ae6ef13ae0b13f19ceb5826a85548464a4ed1610ea55"} {"question" "q-47378" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47378" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2.5\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "2\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "3.5\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "3\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "5\\,\\text{m/s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47378" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2.5\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-71925" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The total number of eggs laid by a pen of chickens on a farm is given by the function $e(t),$ where $t$ is the number of days since the chickens arrived on the farm. The values of $e(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $e(t)$ | $20$ | $45$ | $77$ | $117$ | $160$ | $195$ |\n\nApproximate the rate at which eggs are laid at the $4$th day using a forward difference approximation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-71925" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{e}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{e(t+h)-e(t)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $t=4.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{e(4+1)-e(4)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{e(5)-e(4)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{160-117}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{1} \\\\  & =43.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at $t=4$ the rate at which eggs are laid is approximately $43$ eggs per day." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-71925" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5d7ce2f639b79390f91ae6ef13ae0b13f19ceb5826a85548464a4ed1610ea55"} {"question" "q-71925" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-71925" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$27$ eggs per day"] ["text" "$32$ eggs per day"] ["text" "$38$ eggs per day"] ["text" "$43$ eggs per day"] ["text" "$45$ eggs per day"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-71925" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$43$ eggs per day"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342038" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The volume of water in a tank was recorded over time for $25$ minutes, starting from time $t=0$ minutes. The following table shows the volume $V(t)$, in liters, recorded at time $t.$\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ | $20$ | $25$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $V(t)$ | $120$ | $150$ | $165$ | $180$ | $215$ | $240$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change of volume at time $t=5$ using a forward difference approximation. Do not round the answer.\n\nAt $t=5,$ the estimated rate of change is {{field-1}} liters per minute." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342038" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{V(t+h)-V(t)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $t=5.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(5) & ≈\\frac{V(5+5)-V(5)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{V(10)-V(5)}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{165-150}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{5} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, at $t=5$ the rate of change was approximately $3$ liters per minute." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342038" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5d7ce2f639b79390f91ae6ef13ae0b13f19ceb5826a85548464a4ed1610ea55"} {"question" "q-342038" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342038" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47380" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $g(s)$ describes the amount of fuel, in liters, remaining in Samuel's car after driving $s$ miles. The values of $g(s)$ for different values of $s$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $s$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(s)$ | $15$ | $13.5$ | $12.1$ | $9.4$ | $8.2$ | $6.6$ | $4.8$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change in the fuel volume when $s=8$ miles using a forward difference approximation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47380" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(s)≈\\frac{g(s+h)-f(s)}{h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $s=8.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(8) & ≈\\frac{g(8+2)-g(8)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(10)-g(8)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.6-8.2}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1.6}{2} \\\\  & =-0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at $s=8$ the rate at which the fuel is changing is approximately $-0.8\\,\\text{liters/mile}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47380" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5d7ce2f639b79390f91ae6ef13ae0b13f19ceb5826a85548464a4ed1610ea55"} {"question" "q-47380" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47380" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-0.6\\,\\text{liters/mile}"] ["math" "-0.8\\,\\text{liters/mile}"] ["math" "-1.2\\,\\text{liters/mile}"] ["math" "-1.6\\,\\text{liters/mile}"] ["math" "-2.2\\,\\text{liters/mile}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47380" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-0.8\\,\\text{liters/mile}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/assets/d84ba0a07821011be4cf5958af44ea73e97493c5a796a0324b068fe2adca8a12.png)\n\nA car is using petrol as it travels. The graph of the amount of petrol $P(d)$, in liters, remaining in the fuel tank after traveling a distance of $d$ miles is shown above. Approximate the rate of change of the amount of petrol at $d=150$ using a forward difference with step size $h=50.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of change is negative if the amount of petrol decreases.\n\nAfter traveling $150$ miles, the rate of change of the amount of petrol is {{field-1}} liters per mile." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(d)≈\\frac{P(d+h)-P(d)}{h},\n$$\n\nat the distance $d=150$ with step size $h=50.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(150) & ≈\\frac{P(150+50)-P(150)}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(200)-P(150)}{50} \\\\  & =\\frac{10-30}{50} \\\\  & =-0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after traveling $150$ miles, the rate of change of the amount of petrol is $-0.4$ liters per mile." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5d7ce2f639b79390f91ae6ef13ae0b13f19ceb5826a85548464a4ed1610ea55"} {"question" "q-342075" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342075" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-0.4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-313343" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/assets/204afb23fc438b047c72d6233b59b70c4741ee16a30a57c084682a3d14fd2188.png)\n\nA thin soap film is stretching over time. The graph of its surface area $A(t)$, in square centimeters, after $t$ minutes is shown above. Approximate the rate of change of the surface area at $t=2$ using a forward difference with step size $h=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nAfter $2$ minutes, the surface area increases at a rate of {{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-313343" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{A(t+h)-A(t)}{h},\n$$\n\nat the time $t=2$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{A(2+2)-A(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{A(4)-A(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{40-10}{2} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after $2$ minutes, the surface area changes at a rate of approximately $15\\text{ }{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-313343" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5d7ce2f639b79390f91ae6ef13ae0b13f19ceb5826a85548464a4ed1610ea55"} {"question" "q-313343" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-313343" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "15"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342079" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/assets/846fc347c71ada4c01a5a85aabd0722203b84edbafcbe462064ae31d1cf2adbb.png)\n\nA drone is descending toward the ground. The graph of its height $H(t)$, in meters, after $t$ minutes is shown above. Approximate the rate of change of the height at $t=9$ using a forward difference with step size $h=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of change is negative if the height decreases.\n\nAfter $9$ minutes, the rate of change of the height is {{field-1}} meters per minute." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342079" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{H}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{H(t+h)-H(t)}{h},\n$$\n\nat the time $t=9$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}^{′}(9) & ≈\\frac{H(9+2)-H(9)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{H(11)-H(9)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-80}{2} \\\\  & =-40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after $9$ minutes, the rate of change of the height is $-40$ meters per minute." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342079" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5d7ce2f639b79390f91ae6ef13ae0b13f19ceb5826a85548464a4ed1610ea55"} {"question" "q-342079" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342079" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-40"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/assets/4a7e89a828cbd660357d4834e0bcb9cb5c316dbe24419865206ba5f41872963d.png)\n\nThe power of a mobile phone emitter increases when the phone is switched on. The power output of the emitter after $t$ minutes is given by $W(t),$ where $W$ is measured in watts. The graph of $W(t)$ is shown above. \nApproximate the rate of increase in the power of the emitter when $t=1$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{W(t+h)-W(t)}{h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=1$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{W(1+2)-W(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{W(3)-W(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{100-50}{2} \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after $1$ minute, the power is increasing at a rate of approximately $25\\,\\text{watts}/\\text{minute}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5d7ce2f639b79390f91ae6ef13ae0b13f19ceb5826a85548464a4ed1610ea55"} {"question" "q-47383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12.5\\,\\text{watts}/\\text{minute}"] ["math" "25\\,\\text{watts}/\\text{minute}"] ["math" "37.5\\,\\text{watts}/\\text{minute}"] ["math" "50\\,\\text{watts}/\\text{minute}"] ["math" "75\\,\\text{watts}/\\text{minute}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-47383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "25\\,\\text{watts}/\\text{minute}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/assets/ae57faa04abd053e4a58d751cc084d58e07e84e0520c99a33a122f58dfd8f18c.png)\n\nA slice of jackfruit is placed in an air dryer. The graph of the moisture content $M(t)$, in percent, after $t$ hours is shown above. Approximate the rate of change of the moisture content at $t=3$ using a forward difference with step size $h=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of change is negative if the content decreases.\n\nAfter $3$ hour, the rate of change of moisture content is {{field-1}} percent per hour." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{M(t+h)-M(t)}{h},\n$$\n\nat the time $t=3$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{M(3+2)-M(3)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M(5)-M(3)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{20-40}{2} \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after $3$ hour, the rate of change of moisture content is $-10$ percent per hour." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342080" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f5d7ce2f639b79390f91ae6ef13ae0b13f19ceb5826a85548464a4ed1610ea55"} {"question" "q-342080" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "q-342080" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-10"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/state.json" "file_sha256" "b1b53e298b7988267f7d0c5f1afaa4cc3e6ddc5d21133838fc130bd483344397"} {"question" "e-151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-1.0$ | $-0.7$ | $-0.3$ | $0.7$ | $1.5$ | $2.1$ |\n\nApproximate  at the point $x=3$ using a forward difference approximation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/example-151.json" "file_sha256" "3767d89a888c3f2f41f751c155d3e1fa081c7f374ba6d3cc0bc45b8eef386839"} {"question" "e-151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the forward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\approx \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=3.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{f(3+1)-f(3)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(4)-f(3)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-0.3)-(-0.7)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.4}{1} \\\\  & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/example-151.json" "file_sha256" "3767d89a888c3f2f41f751c155d3e1fa081c7f374ba6d3cc0bc45b8eef386839"} {"question" "e-152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The graph of the function $y=g(x)$ is shown below. \nApproximate $g'(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/assets/3084d6ae67f37517f3d8ad0a9a62be05fe1906fcc2a24f69bd29a1e00dfd0373.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/example-152.json" "file_sha256" "ec19378a91a340308cd490fcd2b9a334d9658f316f7f6911627d243efb7e7e66"} {"question" "e-152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\ng'(x) \\approx \\frac{g(x+h) - g(x)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=4.$  Applying the above formula with $x=4$ and $h=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{g(4+2)-g(4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(6)-g(4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-6}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/example-152.json" "file_sha256" "ec19378a91a340308cd490fcd2b9a334d9658f316f7f6911627d243efb7e7e66"} {"question" "e-153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The position of a race car along a straight track, measured in feet from the starting line, is given by the function $s(t),$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $s(t)$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $12$ | $19$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity $s'(t)$ at the time $t=0.1$ using a forward difference approximation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/example-153.json" "file_sha256" "32b9ea9dbb725470382967c4063fdc551fc53f3f0160ed3891a280fa199894db"} {"question" "e-153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\ns'(t) \\approx \\frac{s(t+h) - s(t)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the time $t=0.1.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}^{′}(0.1) & ≈\\frac{s(0.1+0.1)-s(0.1)}{0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(0.2)-s(0.1)}{0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-3}{0.1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{0.1} \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo at $t=0.1$ the velocity is approximately $40\\, \\textrm{ft}/\\textrm{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/example-153.json" "file_sha256" "32b9ea9dbb725470382967c4063fdc551fc53f3f0160ed3891a280fa199894db"} {"question" "e-154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An oil tank has leaked $q(t)$ liters of oil after $t$ hours. The graph of $q(t)$ is shown below. Approximate the rate at which the oil is leaking from the tank when $t=1$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/assets/b6c5286dae7e739a588173bad9c721ae34d07f86e455e44ec9f229628cf54211.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/example-154.json" "file_sha256" "3c69fe03b631acc1fb4650175437770d618b98975458efa242615cf78b18c19c"} {"question" "e-154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\nq'(t) \\approx \\frac{q(t+h) - q(t)}{h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the time $t=1$ with step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{q(1+1)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{q(2)-q(1)}{1} \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after $1$ hour, the tank is leaking oil at a rate of approximately $1\\,\\textrm{liter}/ \\textrm{hour}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/example-154.json" "file_sha256" "3c69fe03b631acc1fb4650175437770d618b98975458efa242615cf78b18c19c"} {"question" "q-47383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "25\\,\\text{watts}/\\text{minute}"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "f50383dbc512078be25dce58dc958c382573327dd09c1b27103ba390af1a0e57" "reviewer_session" "01a108cd-e7f4-73f3-b8fc-c82135bc6b7d" "rationale" "The original graph gives W(1)=50 and W(3)=100 watts. With the specified h=2 minutes, W′(1)≈(100−50)/2=25 watts/minute, original option a (index 0). The authentic worked solution confirms this. The Incorrect grade concerns the submitted 75 watts/minute. The stored key 25watts/minute agrees with the derived answer." "bindings" {"problem" "d69c3ca71e5dbd9a04b3c2d45bde2974467bd45af6c47410c3e310682b7d29e8" "worked_solution" "eaba269bbb1442adee4ddb8eebb29470860a7e67a86ffd17d070abbed5c8f7a4" "original_field" "fe7a5cb138b7790503f025c8dc1d0e523284ede8b759a99da6ce5f05ad72a466" "reviewed_field" "c016d6c489ac72ccd7f10047dbb998eac70b2ba5ed969f82de20ccb7efd46673" "raw_widget" "d7abee5242356557f32c8c211204ecc2bc4dd7723c817b76213397addaf7f05c"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-47383-before.png" "sha256" "a770ee9e70bb27bbd59d9c1d2f120f463d72bdbeb6dbd514601281c1a1727f46"} {"path" "history-q-47383.png" "sha256" "58ce4a3d7b59e1e33502e3b9741e67fced09fdbf82b226fd86960032f25febdf"} {"path" "q-47383-after.json" "sha256" "952784bef9fc190c3ce1a48409e303a426339ca704f73ef418b98b34b690851d"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "43575edf9e27d94b0b4be753f58364ccc4d5cc357f02f3de6728f408bb5e7fa1"}]} {"question" "q-47383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12.5\\,\\text{watts}/\\text{minute}"] ["math" "25\\,\\text{watts}/\\text{minute}"] ["math" "37.5\\,\\text{watts}/\\text{minute}"] ["math" "50\\,\\text{watts}/\\text{minute}"] ["math" "75\\,\\text{watts}/\\text{minute}"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14057748/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "f50383dbc512078be25dce58dc958c382573327dd09c1b27103ba390af1a0e57" "rationale" "The original graph gives W(1)=50 and W(3)=100 watts. With the specified h=2 minutes, W′(1)≈(100−50)/2=25 watts/minute, original option a (index 0). The authentic worked solution confirms this. The Incorrect grade concerns the submitted 75 watts/minute. The stored key 25watts/minute agrees with the derived answer."}] "usage" {"e-152" [] "e-154" [] "q-47386" [] "e-153" [] "q-47380" [] "e-151" [] "q-47383" []} "basis" 1876 "retractions" [[17592186092667 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/992/Source/Images/q-47386.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y = g(x)$ is shown above.\nApproximate $g^{′}(x)$ at the point $x = 6$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h = 4$."] [17592186092664 :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe function $g(s)$ describes the amount of fuel, in liters, remaining in Samuel's car after driving $s$ miles. The values of $g(s)$ for different values of $s$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $s$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $g(s)$ | $15$ | $13.5$ | $12.1$ | $9.4$ | $8.2$ | $6.6$ | $4.8$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change in the fuel volume when $s = 8$ miles using a forward difference approximation."] [17592186092665 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/992/Source/Images/q-47383.png)\n\nThe power of a mobile phone emitter increases when the phone is switched on. The power output of the emitter after $t$ minutes is given by $W(t)$, where $W$ is measured in watts. The graph of $W(t)$ is shown above.\nApproximate the rate of increase in the power of the emitter when $t = 1$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h = 2$."] [17592186092665 :question/answer-fields 17592186090455] [17592186091902 :question/problem "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $-1.0$ | $-0.7$ | $-0.3$ | $0.7$ | $1.5$ | $2.1$ |\n\nApproximate at the point $x=3$ using a forward difference approximation."] [17592186091902 :question/worked-solution "We use the forward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\approx \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=3$. We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1$. This gives\n\n$$\nf^{′}(3) \\mid \\approx (f(3 + 1) - f(3))/(1); = (f(4) - f(3))/(1); = ((-0.3) - (-0.7))/(1); = \\frac{0.4}{1}; = 0.4\n$$"] [17592186091903 :question/problem "The graph of the function $y=g(x)$ is shown below.\nApproximate $g'(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=2$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/992/Source/Images/f7601d421583e4e520cba5b7a0094276.png)"] [17592186091903 :question/worked-solution "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\ng'(x) \\approx \\frac{g(x+h) - g(x)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=4$. Applying the above formula with $x=4$ and $h=2$ gives\n\n$$\ng^{′}(4) \\mid \\approx (g(4 + 2) - g(4))/(2); = (g(6) - g(4))/(2); = \\frac{2 - 6}{2}; = \\frac{-4}{2}; =-2\n$$"] [17592186091904 :question/problem "The position of a race car along a straight track, measured in feet from the starting line, is given by the function $s(t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $s(t)$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $12$ | $19$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity $s'(t)$ at the time $t=0.1$ using a forward difference approximation."] [17592186091904 :question/worked-solution "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\ns'(t) \\approx \\frac{s(t+h) - s(t)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the time $t=0.1$. We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.1$. This gives\n\n$$\ns^{′}(0.1) \\mid \\approx (s(0.1 + 0.1) - s(0.1))/(0.1); = (s(0.2) - s(0.1))/(0.1); = \\frac{7 - 3}{0.1}; = \\frac{4}{0.1}; = 40\n$$\n\nSo at $t=0.1$ the velocity is approximately $40\\, \\textrm{ft}/\\textrm{s}$."] [17592186091905 :question/problem "An oil tank has leaked $q(t)$ liters of oil after $t$ hours. The graph of $q(t)$ is shown below. Approximate the rate at which the oil is leaking from the tank when $t=1$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/992/Source/Images/b42fd1d22af4ee74a06a5344ab52a8c8.png)"] [17592186091905 :question/worked-solution "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\nq'(t) \\approx \\frac{q(t+h) - q(t)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the time $t=1$ with step size $h=1$. This gives\n\n$$\nq^{′}(1) \\mid \\approx (q(1 + 1) - q(1))/(1); = (q(2) - q(1))/(1); = 3 - 2; = 1\n$$\n\nSo, after $1$ hour, the tank is leaking oil at a rate of approximately $1\\,\\textrm{liter}/ \\textrm{hour}$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186090450 17592186090451 17592186090452 17592186090453 17592186090454 17592186090456 17592186090457 17592186090458 17592186090459 17592186090460 17592186090468 17592186090469 17592186090470 17592186090471 17592186090472] "immutable_fields" [[17592186090449 :answer-field/correct] [17592186090449 :answer-field/id] [17592186090449 :answer-field/key] [17592186090449 :answer-field/type] [17592186090455 :answer-field/correct] [17592186090455 :answer-field/id] [17592186090455 :answer-field/key] [17592186090455 :answer-field/type] [17592186090467 :answer-field/correct] [17592186090467 :answer-field/id] [17592186090467 :answer-field/key] [17592186090467 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
